    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0000", "title": "https://eservices.zatca.gov.sa/sites/sc/en/pages/home.aspx  https://www.saso.gov", "content": "https://eservices.zatca.gov.sa/sites/sc/en/pages/home.aspx  https://www.saso.gov.sa/en/laws-and-regulations/technical_regulations/pages/default.aspx  https://www.saso.gov.sa/ar/Laws-And-Regulations/Technical_regulations/Documents/TR-Textile-Products.pdf  https://www.akingump.com/en/insights/alerts/kingdom-of-saudi-arabias-new-personal-data-protection-law-and-implementing-regulations-key-obligations-responsibilities-and-rights  Kingdom of Saudi Arabia’s New Personal Data Protection Law and Implementing Regulations—Key Obligations, Responsibilities and Rights  October 20, 2023  By: Natasha G. Ko", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0001", "title": "On September 7, 2023, the Saudi Authority for Data and Artificial Intelligence (", "content": "On September 7, 2023, the Saudi Authority for Data and Artificial Intelligence (SDAIA) issued the Implementing Regulations of the Personal Data Protection Law (the Implementing Regulations) and the Regulations on Personal Data Transfer outside the Geographical Boundaries of the Kingdom (the Data Transfer Regulations, together, the Regulations). The Regulations mark an important development in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia’s (KSA) data protection landscape as they set out helpful clarity and significant detail supplementing the KSA Personal Data Protection Law (PDPL), the KSA’s first comprehensiv", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0002", "title": "The Regulations (issued one week prior to the enforcement date of the PDPL) expa", "content": "The Regulations (issued one week prior to the enforcement date of the PDPL) expand upon the provisions of the PDPL and outline additional compliance obligations. We set out below the key features of the Regulations.  1. Implementing Regulations  The PDPL regulates the processing of personal data relating to an individual in the KSA by any means, including where such processing is conducted by a party outside the KSA, and further establishes certain novel rights for individuals in relation to how their personal data is processed. The Implementing Regulations clarify and supplement the PDPL. Key", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0003", "title": "Data Subject Rights: Data controllers are obligated to act on a request from a d", "content": "Data Subject Rights: Data controllers are obligated to act on a request from a data subject within 30 days (except in certain instances in which this period may be extended by an additional 30 days, e.g., if the data controller receives multiple requests from the data subject) and to provide appropriate means for requests to be processed. While Article 4 of the PDPL outlines the various rights available to data subjects, the Implementing Regulations now provide further detail and clarity, including:", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0004", "title": "Right to be Informed: The Implementing Regulations differentiate between instanc", "content": "Right to be Informed: The Implementing Regulations differentiate between instances in which data is collected (A) directly from a data subject and (B) from an individual other than the data subject. In respect of (A), a data controller is required to take the “necessary measures” to inform data subjects of prescribed information, including the legal basis and a “specific, clear, and explicit purpose” for the processing. The PDPL specifically requires that a data controller use a privacy policy to make certain information available to data subjects.  In respect of (B), a data controller shall “", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0005", "title": "Right of Access and to Request a Copy: The Implementing Regulations note that th", "content": "Right of Access and to Request a Copy: The Implementing Regulations note that the right to access and to request a copy of personal data in a “readable and clear format” and a “commonly used electronic format” (although the data subject may request a printed hard copy if feasible) are subject to certain conditions, including that exercising the right should not negatively impact the rights of others. Data controllers are obligated to ensure that they do not disclose the identity of another individual when granting access to the data.  Right to Restrict Processing: The Implementing Regulations ", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0006", "title": "Right to Request Destruction: The Implementing Regulations set out circumstances", "content": "Right to Request Destruction: The Implementing Regulations set out circumstances in which a data controller shall be required to destroy personal data, such as upon the exercise of a data subject’s rights, where the personal data is no longer necessary to achieve the purpose for which it was collected, or if the data controller becomes aware that the data is being processed in violation of the PDPL. The Implementing Regulations also prescribe the steps a data controller must take when destroying personal data.", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0007", "title": "Anonymization: The Implementing Regulations impose obligations on data controlle", "content": "Anonymization: The Implementing Regulations impose obligations on data controllers when anonymizing personal data, including ensuring that it is “impossible” to re-identify a data subject after the anonymization and requiring that the data controller evaluate the impact and the effectiveness of the techniques applied for anonymization. The data controller must take the necessary organizational, administrative and technical measures to avoid risks identified and make the necessary adjustments to ensure re-identification is not possible. The Implementation Regulations further confirm that anonym", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0008", "title": "Consent: Consent is at the forefront of the PDPL. The Implementing Regulations c", "content": "Consent: Consent is at the forefront of the PDPL. The Implementing Regulations confirm that consent for the processing of personal data can be in “any appropriate form or means, including written or verbal consent or by using electronic methods,” subject to certain conditions. For example, the consent must be documented in a manner that allows verification in the future and independent consent should be obtained for each processing purpose. Furthermore, consent must be explicit in certain instances, including where the processing involves sensitive data or credit data. The Implementing Regulat", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0009", "title": "Legitimate Interest: The Implementation Regulations stipulate the conditions und", "content": "Legitimate Interest: The Implementation Regulations stipulate the conditions under which a data controller may process data on the basis of legitimate interests, including that the purpose must not violate any KSA laws, that there be a balance between the rights and interests of the data subject and the legitimate interests of the data controller, and that such processing be within the “reasonable expectation” of the data subject. Importantly, before processing data on the basis of legitimate interests, the Implementing Regulations require a data controller to conduct and document an assessmen", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0010", "title": "Data Processors: The Implementing Regulations provide helpful detail regarding t", "content": "Data Processors: The Implementing Regulations provide helpful detail regarding the selection of data processors and sub-processors, including noting that any data processor chosen must provide sufficient guarantees to protect personal data. The Implementing Regulations further set out the information that must be contained in a data processing agreement, such as the purpose of processing and a clarification as to whether the data processor is subject to regulations in other countries that may impact their compliance with the PDPL. Furthermore, data controllers shall be responsible for periodic", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0011", "title": "Disclosure of Personal Data: The Implementing Regulations address data controlle", "content": "Disclosure of Personal Data: The Implementing Regulations address data controllers’ obligations in response to a request from a public authority, including requiring that controllers document the request for disclosure. In addition, except as provided in the PDPL, when disclosing data to a third party, the Implementing Regulations require the data controller to take “necessary care and provide sufficient guarantees,” such as ensuring the pseudonymization of personal data.", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0012", "title": "Personal Data Breaches: The Implementing Regulations provide that data controlle", "content": "Personal Data Breaches: The Implementing Regulations provide that data controllers must take the necessary organizational, administrative and technical measures to ensure the privacy of the data subject and the security of personal data. A data controller must notify the competent authority within 72 hours of becoming aware of a data breach if such incident potentially causes harm to the personal data or the data subject or conflicts with the data subject’s rights or interests. The Implementing Regulations specify the details that must be included in the notification, including a description o", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0013", "title": "Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA): The Implementing Regulations set out t", "content": "Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA): The Implementing Regulations set out the circumstances in which a DPIA may be required, including in respect of the processing of sensitive personal data or where the data controller is providing a product or service that involves processing data likely to cause “serious harm” to the privacy of data subjects. The Implementing Regulations specify the minimum requirements of a DPIA and confirm that a controller must provide a copy of the DPIA to any processor acting on its behalf in relation to the relevant processing.", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0014", "title": "Advertising and Direct Marketing: The Implementing Regulations confirm that a da", "content": "Advertising and Direct Marketing: The Implementing Regulations confirm that a data controller must obtain consent before sending advertising or awareness material (which is not further defined in the PDPL or the Regulations) in cases where there is no “prior interaction” between the data controller and the targeted recipient. There are also specific conditions for obtaining consent in these circumstances. Similarly, the Implementing Regulations require that a controller obtain consent from a data subject prior to processing data for direct marketing purposes.", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0015", "title": "Data Protection Officer (DPO): The Implementing Regulations stipulate that (i) a", "content": "Data Protection Officer (DPO): The Implementing Regulations stipulate that (i) a data controller must appoint one or more individuals to be responsible for the protection of personal data in certain circumstances (including where the primary activities of the data controller consist of processing that requires regular and continuous monitoring of individuals on a large scale or involve processing sensitive personal data) and (ii) such person may be an employee or an external contractor. The Implementing Regulations also clarify the responsibilities of a DPO.", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0016", "title": "Records of Processing Activities (ROPA): Under the Implementing Regulations, dat", "content": "Records of Processing Activities (ROPA): Under the Implementing Regulations, data controllers are required to keep a written, accurate and up to date ROPA during the period of its data processing activities in addition to five years from the date of completion of the processing. The Implementing Regulations outline the minimum information that must be included in the ROPA and further note that the competent authority shall provide a template ROPA.", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0017", "title": "Other Key Features: The Implementing Regulations contain specific provisions app", "content": "Other Key Features: The Implementing Regulations contain specific provisions applicable to the processing of health data, the processing of credit data, the processing of data for scientific, research or statistical purposes, and the role and obligations of legal guardians. The Implementing Regulations state that the competent authority shall issue rules regarding registration in a National Register of Controllers and shall specify which data controllers are required to register. Data subjects may also file a complaint with the competent authority within 90 days from the date of an incident.  ", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0018", "title": "Article 2 of the Data Transfer Regulations sets out certain overarching provisio", "content": "Article 2 of the Data Transfer Regulations sets out certain overarching provisions, including that any transfer or disclosure of personal data outside the KSA should be limited to the minimum amount necessary to achieve the purpose of the transfer and requiring that the data controller ensure the transfer does not impact the privacy of data subjects or the level of protection guaranteed under the PDPL.", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0019", "title": "The Data Transfer Regulations provide for the competent authority (to be determi", "content": "The Data Transfer Regulations provide for the competent authority (to be determined by a resolution of the Council of Ministers) to coordinate with the relevant authorities in the KSA to conduct an assessment of the level of protection for personal data in jurisdictions outside the KSA and to submit the results of the assessment to the Prime Minister. The competent authority also needs to submit their recommendation as to whether the Prime Minister ought to issue an adequacy decision, to enter into an international agreement with the relevant jurisdiction, or to do neither. The Data Transfer R", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0020", "title": "Articles 5 through 7 of the Data Transfer Regulations clarify the circumstances ", "content": "Articles 5 through 7 of the Data Transfer Regulations clarify the circumstances in which a data controller may transfer personal data outside the KSA in the absence of an adequacy decision. Article 5 of the Data Transfer Regulations stipulates that in the absence of an adequacy decision, a data controller may transfer data outside the KSA if “appropriate safeguards” are in place (provided the regulatory requirements in the relevant country does not prejudice the privacy of data subjects or the ability to enforce appropriate safeguards). The Data Transfer Regulations state that “appropriate saf", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0021", "title": "In the event that a data controller is unable to use any of the data transfer sa", "content": "In the event that a data controller is unable to use any of the data transfer safeguards specified in Article 5 (and in the absence of an adequacy decision), Article 6 provides that the transfer of personal data outside the KSA may be permitted only (i) where the transfer is necessary for the performance of an agreement to which the data subject is party or (ii) if the transfer is necessary to protect the vital interests of a data subject that is “unreachable.” If the data controller is a public entity, it has two additional grounds on which it could rely: where the transfer is necessary for t", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0022", "title": "Even if a data controller can transfer data in accordance with Article 5 or 6, t", "content": "Even if a data controller can transfer data in accordance with Article 5 or 6, the Data Transfer Regulations note that such transfers must “immediately” cease in certain circumstances, such as if the transfer affects the national security or vital interests of the KSA or the appropriate safeguards adopted are no longer applicable. Furthermore, a data controller is required to conduct a risk assessment of the cross-border transfer when the transfer occurs pursuant to Article 5 or 6 or involves continuous or large-scale transfers of sensitive data outside the KSA. The Data Transfer Regulations d", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "V3-pdpl_p001_c0023", "title": "Both the Data Transfer Regulations and the Implementation Regulations entered in", "content": "Both the Data Transfer Regulations and the Implementation Regulations entered into force from the date of the PDPL’s enforcement, namely September 14, 2023. As the PDPL is now in force, businesses that are required to comply with the PDPL should promptly start examining their data processing activities, including any cross-border data transfers, to ensure timely compliance with the PDPL. Please see our previous blog post regarding actions that businesses can take. If you need assistance with complying with the new PDPL, please do not hesitate to reach out to our team for support and guidance.", "source": "KSA_PDPL.docx", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-001", "title": "1 ", "content": "1 \n CAPITAL MARKET AUTHORITY  \n \nAnti -Money Laundering and  \nCounter -Terrorist Financing Rules  \n \nEnglish Translation of the Official Arabic Text  \nIssued by the Board of the Capital Market Authority  \nPursuant to its Resolution Number (1-39-2008 ) \nDated  3/12/ 1429 H  Corre sponding to  1/12/2008  \nBased on the Capital Market Law  \nissued by Royal Decree No. M/30 dated 2/6/1424H  \nAmended by Resolution of the Board of the Capital Market Authority Number  \n (1-85-2017 ) Dated  27/12/1438H Corresponding to 18/09/2017G      \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nArabic is the official language of the ", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-002", "title": "2 ", "content": "2 \n Anti-Money Laundering and  \nCounter -Terrorist Financing Rules  \n \n \nGENERAL PROVISIONS  \n \nPART 1  \n \nArticle 1:  \n \nPreliminary  \n \nThe objectives  of these Rules  \n \nThe objectives  of these Rules are that all authorised and registered persons must \ncomply fully with the controls and procedures issued by the Capital Mark et Authority \nto ensure that:  \n \n (a). fully apply the Anti-Money Laundering Law  issued by Royal Decree No. M / 31 \ndated 11/05/1433 H and its implementing Regulatio n, and  apply the requirements \nof the FATF\'s 40 Recommendations and 9 Special Recommendations dea lin", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-003", "title": "3 ", "content": "3 \n in an actual transaction; giving advice shall also con stitute establishing a business \nrelationship.   \n \nClient – refers to a customer, execution -only customer either natural or legal person \nand counterparty for whom an Authorised Person executes securities business .   \n \nCounterparty  – a client who is an authoris ed person, an exempt person, an institution \nor a non -Saudi financial services firm . \n \nFIU the Financial Intelligence  Unit mentioned in  the Anti -Money Laundering Law \nissued by Royal Decree No. M  / 31 dated 11/5/1433H  and its implementing \nRegulation . \n \nFunds - me", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-004", "title": "4 ", "content": "4 \n 2-   Without prejudice to paragraph (1) of this article, expressions and terms in these Rules \nhave the meaning s which they bear in the Capital Market Law and in the Glossary of \ndefined terms used in the Regulatio ns and Rules of the Capital Market Authority.", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-005", "title": "5 ", "content": "5 \n PART2  \n \nGeneral application of AML/CFT requirements  \n \nArticle 3:  \n \nGeneral principles  \n \n1. Authorised Person must , in establishing policies  and procedures  to prevent  \nmoney l aundering and terrorist financing, consider carefully the specific \nnature of its business, organisational structure, type of client  and transaction,  \nand shall  ensure  that the measures taken by it are adequate and appropriate to \nmeet the requirements and ge neral objectives set out in these Rules.  \n \n2. Saudi Arabia has ratified and implemented the United Nations Convention on \nIllicit Traffic in Na", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-006", "title": "6 ", "content": "6 \n checking of the adequacy of reports generated on large and / or irregular \ntransactions; (iii) review of the quality of reporting of suspicious \ntransactions; and (iv) an assessment of the level of awareness of front line \nstaff regarding their responsibilities) ; and  \n \n(d) adopt client acceptance policies and procedures, and undertake required \nCustomer Due D iligence (“CDD”) measures, as set out in Part (3), \nincluding taking into account the risk of money laundering and terrorist \nfinancing depending on the type of client, business relationship or \ntransaction.  \n \nArticle 4:  \n \nAppl", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-007", "title": "7 ", "content": "7 \n PART 3  \n \nCUSTOMER  DUE D ILIGENCE  \n \nArticle 6: \n \nFor the purposes of these rules and before accepting any client, the authorised person \nmust prepare a \"Know Your Customer\" form containing the information required by \nAnnex (5 -3) of the authorised persons regulations and the other inf ormation required \nby these rules.  \n \nArticle 7: \n \nClient Acceptance  \n \n1. Authorised Person must develop client acceptance policies and procedures that \naim to identify the types of clients that are likely to pose a higher risk of money \nlaundering and terrorist fi nancing. A more extensive customer", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-008", "title": "8 ", "content": "8 \n Article 8 \n \nCustomer Due Diligence (CDD) – general  \n \n \n1. Authorised Person must take all steps necessary to be able to establish the true \nand full identity of each client, and of each client’s financial situation and \ninvestment objectives. Authorised Person must not open anonymous accounts, \naccounts using fals e or fictitious names, or accounts for prohibited persons \nnotified by the Authority .   \n \n2.  CDD must be carried out on all clients, and CDD requires Authorised Person to \ntake the following steps:  \n \n(a) Identify the client  and verify their identity using the  original d", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-009", "title": "9 ", "content": "9 \n  (b) Legal persons:  \n \nFor all c lients that are legal persons, A uthorised Person must obtain sufficient \ninformation about the nature of the business and its ownership and control structure so \nthat it can identify the individual(s) that ultimately own(s) or control(s) the client . \nSpecimen signatures shall be obtained for all account signatories.  \n \n- Companies:  \n A copy of Commercial register issued by the competent authority.  \n A copy of the Articles of association, or the memorandum of  association and their \nannexes, and any amendment s \n A copy of the identification card of", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-010", "title": "10 ", "content": "10 \n 6. If there is doubt or difficulty in determining whether the document obtained to \nverify identity is genuine, Authorised Persons must not open the account, and \nshall consider whether they need to make a suspicious transaction  report.  \n \n7. Authori sed P erson must retain copies of all documents used to verify the \nidentity of the client  pursuant to Part (4) from these rules , \n \n8. For the purpose of assisting an Authorised Person to identify the beneficial \nowner of an account, the Authorised Person must, when establishing a business \nrelationship, ask whether the client is acting ", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-011", "title": "11 ", "content": "11 \n 3. Authorised Person must establish clearly in its client acceptanc e policies the risk \nfactors for determining what types of clients and activities are to be considered \nas high risk, while recognising that no policy can be exhaustive in setting out all \nrisk factors that must be considered in every possible situation , such risk factors \nmust include client risk, country , geographic risk and product/service risk .  \n4. In assessing whether or not a country sufficiently applies the FATF standards in \ncombating money laundering and terrorist financing, Authoris ed Person must:  \n \n(a)  ", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-012", "title": "12 ", "content": "12 \n (e)     obtaining  the approval of senior management of the Authorised Person \nwhen opening an accoun t. \n \nArticle 10:    \n \nPolitically exposed persons  (PEP s) \n \n1. Authorised Person  shall have risk management systems in place to identify whether \na client or  potential client, or a beneficial owner, is a PEP. Any accounts identified \nas being held by such persons shall be considered higher risk and Authorised \nPerson  shall conduct enhanced ongoing monitoring of such accounts.  \n \n2. The opening or continued operation of an account for a PEP must be approved by \nsenior management  o", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-013", "title": "13 ", "content": "13 \n 2. In general, an Authorised Person must verify the identity of the client  or \npotential client  and beneficial owner before or during the course of establishing \nthe business relationship. When the Author ised Person is unable to perform the \nCDD process satisfactorily at the account opening stage, it must terminate the \nbusiness relationship and not  perform any transaction , and must consider \nwhether a Suspicious Transaction Report  (STR)  must be made .  \n \nArticle 1 3: \n \nInvestment Funds  \n \n1. Wher e Authorised Person act for a client  who is investing in an investment fund \nor a", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-014", "title": "14 ", "content": "14 \n 4. Prior to reliance, Authorised Person must satisfy itself  that it is rea sonable to \nrely on a third party to apply the CDD and that the CDD measures are as \nrigorous  as those which the Authorised Person would have conducted itself for \nthe client. Authorised Persons must establish clear policies in order to \ndetermine whether the third party in question possesses an acceptable level of \nreliability.  \n \n5. Authorised Person relying upon a third party  must:  \n \n(a) obtain copies of the CDD documentation and information , as required by \nparagraphs ( 2/a) to ( 2/c) of Article (8) of t", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-015", "title": "15 ", "content": "15 \n  \n \n \n \nArticle 1 6: \n \nNon-face to face business relationships  \n \n1.  An Authorised Person shall consider money laundering and terrorist financing \nthreats that may arise from the misuse of new or developing technologies, and \nmust formulate its po licies, procedures and controls, to prevent  such threats.  \n \n2. An Authorised Person must formulate its policies, procedures and controls in a \nway that addresses the specific risks associated with non -face to face business \nrelationships and transactions. The  specific risks associated with non -face to \nface business relationships must b", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-016", "title": "16 ", "content": "16 \n Article 1 8: \n \nReview and updating of records  \n \nIdentification data collected under the CDD process mu st be kept up -to-date, accurate \nand relevant. Authorised Person must undertake annual  or ad hoc reviews of existing \nrecords, particularly for higher risk categories of clients or business relationships, \nwhen appropriate trigger events occur.  Examples of t rigger events that might prompt \nan Authorised Person to seek appropriate updated information include:  \n \n an existing client applying to open a new account or establish a new \nrelationship, or significantly alter the nature ", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-017", "title": "17 ", "content": "17 \n PART 4  \n \nRecord Keeping  \n \nArticle 1 9: \n \nRecord Keeping Requirements  \n \n1. Authorised Person shall  ensure to  comply with the record keeping requiremen ts \ncontained in the relevant rules & regulations of the Authority and the company  \nSaudi Stock Exchange (Tadawul) , and must keep a record of all client \nidentification data and other information and documents obtained as part of the \nCDD process, account fil es and business correspondence, as well as all \ntransaction records.  \n \n2. Authorised Person shall maintain  sufficient records to permit reconstruction of \nindividual trans", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-018", "title": "18 ", "content": "18 \n PART 5  \n \nSuspicious Transaction Report  \n \nArticle 20: \n \nSuspicious Transaction Report  \n \n1. Consistent with the obligations set out in the AML Law and  its implementing \nRegulations, Authorised Person must immediately report to the FIU any complex, \nhuge or unusual transaction or raises doubt and suspicion concerning its nature \nand purpose, or  is related to money laundering, financing of terrorism, terrorist \nacts, or terrorist organizations .  \n \n2.  Within 10 days the Authorised Person must submit a detailed  report setting out all \navailable data and information about the suspic", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-019", "title": "19 ", "content": "19 \n transaction to the FIU, the reasons for this shall be fully documented by the \nMLRO.  \n \n7. Authorised Person must keep records of all transactions referred to the MLRO, \ntogether with all internal findings and analysis don e in relation to them.   A register \nmust be maintained of all STR made to the FIU as well as of all reports made by \nemployees to the MLRO, including those where a decision is made by the MLRO \nnot to report to the FIU.  \n \n8. Authorised Person shall use the sta ndard form prescribed by the FIU for reporting, \nand must report by fax, email or any other means agreed by", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-020", "title": "20 ", "content": "20 \n Article 2 1: \n \nTipping Off  \n \n1. In all circumstances, Authorised Person, its directors, officers and employees \nmust ensure that clients or related parties are not alerted to the fact that they \nhave made or are considering making an STR or providing docum ents or \ninformation linked to an STR to the FIU. An Authorised Person shall at all times \nkeep its reporting of suspicious transactions highly confidential, and reports of \nsuspicious transactions or reports that are to be reviewed by the MLRO for \npossible r eporting shall be accessible only by specifically authoris ed staff of the", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-021", "title": "21 ", "content": "21 \n made by the competent authority to release funds for legal or living expenses, \nthen the Authority will notify the Authorised Person.", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-022", "title": "22 ", "content": "22 \n PART 6  \n \nInternal policies, procedures and controls  \n \nArticle 2 3: \n \nInternal Policies and Compliance  \n \n1. Authorised Person shall develop and implement internal policies, procedures \nand controls to help prevent money laundering and  terrorist financing and must \ncommunicate these to its employees. The compliance officer shall also ensure \ncompliance with the AML/CFT policies, procedures and controls .  \n \n2. The policies, procedures and controls must include, amongst other things, CDD \nmeasures, record retention, the detection of unusual and/or suspicious \ntransactions and the ob", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-023", "title": "23 ", "content": "23 \n Article 2 5: \n \n \nEducation and Training  \n \n1. Authorised Person must take all appropriate steps to ensure that their staffs \n(group -wide) receive regular training on:  \n \n(a) AML/CFT  agreements,  laws and instructions , and in particular, CDD \nmeasures, detecting and reporting of suspicious transactions;  \n(b) prevailing techniques, methods and trends in money laundering and \nterrorist financing; and  \n(c) the Authorised Person’s int ernal policies, procedures and controls on \nAML/CFT and the roles and responsibilities of staff in combating money \nlaundering and terrorist financing.  ", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-024", "title": "24 ", "content": "24 \n PART 7 \n \nClosing Provisions  \nArticle 2 6: \n \nSanctions  \n \nWithout pr ejudice to the sanctions imposed under AML Law, A uthoris ed Person or \nany director, manager or employee of an Authoris ed Person that fails to comply with \nany article of these Rules shall be subject to the sanctions set out in Articles 59 and 62 \nof the Capit al Market Law.  \n \nArticle 2 7:  \n \nPublication and Entry into Force   \n \nThese Rules  shall become effective upon their publication.", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-025", "title": "25 ", "content": "25 \n Annex 1 \n \nSecurities Business Indicators for Money \nLaundering or Terrorist Financing      \n  \n \n \nExampl es of possible indicators that a transaction or activity may be linked to money \nlaundering or terrorist financing are as follows:  \n \n \n1. The client exhibits unusual concern regarding the firm\'s compliance with \nAML/CFT requirements, particularly with respect to his identity and type of \nbusiness.  \n \n2. Client refuses to identify himself or to indicate legitimate sources for his funds \nand other assets.  \n \n3. The client wishes to engage in transactions that have no apparent legal", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-AML_CTF_Rules_Issued-026", "title": "26 ", "content": "26 \n  \n11. The client make s a long -term investment followed shortly thereafter by a \nrequest to liquidate the position an d transfer s the proceeds out of the account.   \n \n12. The client’s activities vary substantially from normal practices.  \n \n13. A client refuses to provide the Authorised Person with basic information for a \nmutual fund to verify the client\'s identity.  \n \n14. The client requ ests that the Authori sed Person uses wire transfers in such a \nmanner that originator information is not transferred from the sending to the \nreceiving destination.  \n \n15. A client seeks to change", "source": "compliance/AML CTF Rules Issued by CMA.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-001", "title": "HOW TO START SELLING   ", "content": "HOW TO START SELLING   \nON AMAZON IN SAUDI ARABIA (SA)\nYour journey to successful selling in our SA Store\nThis checklist is designed to help guide your understanding of our SA store and ensure \nyou are set up for success. Be sure to familiarize yourself with all the tools Amazon \nprovides, which \nwill make many of the requirements listed below simpler and easier \nto manage.\nPrepare to sell\nLearn about growth opportunity in our SA store and how customers are shopping\nLearn about the diﬀerent products you can sell in our SA Store and understand which categories are      \nrestricted or require ap", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-002", "title": "2. Learn about the diﬀerent products you can sell in our SA store and", "content": "2. Learn about the diﬀerent products you can sell in our SA store and\nunderstand which categories are restricted or require approval.\nMake sure you are allowed to sell the product. Understand your distribution rights. Also, be \naware that safety and compliance regulations in Saudi Arabia diﬀer from those in other \ngeographic regions.\nLearn which product categories are open to start selling in our SA store. This video shows \nyou how to ﬁnd out if you can sell your product category in our \nSA store.\n1 Source: ecommerceDB.com, 2021\n2 Source : ecommerceDB.com, 2021\n3Sources: Statista Digital Marke", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-003", "title": "REGISTER AND LIST ", "content": "REGISTER AND LIST \n3.\nUnderstand how selling on our SA store works and learn what\ninformation you need to provide during registration.\nNew to Selling on\n our SA Amazon store? \nSelling on our SA store is an eﬃcient and eﬀective way to reach millions of potential \nnew customers. \n1. Register on sellercentral.amazon.sa (free of charge for a limited time)\n2. Submit your documents (ID, Bank statement)\n3. Finalize your SA Seller Central Account Setup\n4. Check for restricted product categories and legal restrictions\n5. Upload your listings\n6. Select a shipping carrier and start using FBA right away\n7", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-004", "title": "Bank account or credit card statement that contain your credentials ", "content": "Bank account or credit card statement that contain your credentials \nDocument must meet the following requirements: \n• Scan or take a picture of the original document in color using your mobile device. If you are  downloading\na statement from online, it must be in PDF format. Do not submit in Excel format or a  screenshot. (Even\nif converted into a PDF)\n•Your document must be in one of the following languages: Arabic, Chinese, English, French, German,\nItalian, \nJapanese, Portuguese, or Spanish. If it is not in one of these languages, provide a notarized\ntranslation \nof your document into one o", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-005", "title": "5. Understand the listing tools available in our SA store and learn more about h", "content": "5. Understand the listing tools available in our SA store and learn more about how \nBuild International Listings (BIL) helps you manage your listings\nListing in small batches or in bulk \nYou can list a small number of products using the Add-a-Product  tool, or you can use \ninventory ﬁles to list your products in bulk. Inventory ﬁle templates let you to add or edit \nproduct listings in bulk. You can create a template to suit your speciﬁc requirements and list \ndiﬀerent types of products across multiple categories using one template.\nBuild International Listings \nThe Build International Listings", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-006", "title": "SHIP AND FU LFILL", "content": "SHIP AND FU LFILL\n6. Understand fulﬁllment options in our SA store and choose between Fulﬁllment \nby Amazon or self-fulﬁllment for your products.\nDeliver your products to the customers\nYou can ship and fulﬁll the products you sell on our SA  store from your own local inventory, \nor you can choose Fulﬁllment by Amazon (FBA). Note that if you choose to manage your own \nshipping, you are responsible for the customer experience from shipping to returns.    Please \nuse international carriers and select duty delivery paid (DDP) incoterms. When fulfilling \nyourself, please make sure you provide a Val", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-007", "title": "FBA New Selection programme (Limited time offer for eligibile New-to-", "content": "FBA New Selection programme (Limited time offer for eligibile New-to-\nFBA ASINs)\nYou can participate in the Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) New selection program, and qualify to \nget free monthly storage for all eligible, new-to-FBA parent ASINs. To participate, you must \nenroll in the program. After you enroll, we will determine whether you qualify for the fee \nwaivers based on the eligibility criteria.\nProgram details\n•Limited to new-to-FBA parent ASINs: Once you enroll in the program, you can use the fee\nwaiver benefits for an unlimited number of new-to-FBA parent ASINs that you create.\n•Free m", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-008", "title": "7. Understand international fulﬁllment requirements,", "content": "7. Understand international fulﬁllment requirements,\nincluding import and export regulations\nGetting your products to \nSaudi Arabia\nUsing FBA in our SA store is a similar process once your items are \nin Amazon’s warehouses, but getting the items \nto those warehouses in Jeddah and Rizadh has some unique diﬀerences \nthan the process in the EU/UK. Amazon’s Service Provider \nNetwork  consists of independent third party providers and \ncan be a good starting point to help you ﬁnd out the right \nservice for your business needs. \nRegardless of which fulﬁllment method you choose, shipping to Saudi Arab", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-009", "title": "MANAGE YO UR BUSINESS", "content": "MANAGE YO UR BUSINESS\n9. Understand tax and regulation requirements when you start selling\nVAT is an indirect tax that applies to sales and purchases. The VAT Rates may vary \ndepending on the destination country and items being delivered. The rate of VAT that \napplies to most supplies of goods and services in Saudi Arabia is 15%.\nIf you are a not a resident in Saudi Arabia, supplying goods in Saudi Arabia through \nAmazon\'s FBA channel, then you will be required to register for VAT irrespective of the \nlevel of turnover from such sales. VAT registration will be effective from the date on \nwhich", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-010", "title": "10. Manage customer support in the local language and either provide a local  ", "content": "10. Manage customer support in the local language and either provide a local  \naddress for returns or oﬀer a refund for international shipping. (FBA manages  \nfulﬁllment and returns questions.)\nCustomer support \nLocal language customer support: When you choose to fulﬁll customer orders yourself, \nit’s not just the picking, packing, and shipping to customers that you’ll need to handle; \nAmazon also requires that you handle customer support. \nTimely responses: Customers expect prompt, helpful service when they have questions or \nconcerns about your products. To maintain a healthy seller scorecar", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-011", "title": "11. Promote your products to increase your sales potential to customers in  our ", "content": "11. Promote your products to increase your sales potential to customers in  our \nSA store\nRaise your visibility with Sponsored Products \nAdvertising on our SA store is an easy way for you to promote your listings. Ads appear \nprominently where customers will see them, such as on the ﬁrst page of search results or \nproduct detail pages. You place bids on relevant keywords, and if your bid wins and your \nad matches the search, your ad gets displayed to shoppers. These targeted ads can help \nyou increase sales by showcasing your products to a wider audience. \nWe have two eﬀective advertising solu", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide-012", "title": "Register your brand ", "content": "Register your brand \nYou can protect your registered trademarks on Amazon through Amazon Brand Registry \nto create a more accurate and trusted experience for customers. Amazon Brand Registry gives you access to powerful brand-management tools, including proprietary text and image search, predictive automation based on your reports of suspected intellectual property rights violations, and increased authority over product listings with your brand name. See how to get started now. \nCoupons\nWe recommend to use coupons to drive visibility of your newly launched selection. Helping you to diﬀerentiat", "source": "compliance/Amazon_SA_VAT_Guide.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-001", "title": "1 ", "content": "1 \n Anti -Money Laundering Law  \n \nI. Definitions  \nArticle 1  \nThe following terms and phrases – wherever mentioned in this Law – shall have the meanings \nassigned thereto unless the context requires otherwise:  \n \n1- The law : the A nti-Money Laundering  law. \n2- The regulation : the Implementing R egulation of this law.   \n3- Funds: Assets , economic resources  or properties of any value or type,  however acquired,  \nwhether material or immaterial, movable or immovable,  tangible or intangible,  along with \ndocuments, deeds , transfers , letter of credits  and instruments of any form, wheth", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-002", "title": "2 ", "content": "2 \n 13- Competent Authority:  Any administrative authorit y, law -enforcement a uthority  or \nsupervisory authorit y. \n14- Bearer Negotiable Instruments: Monetary instruments in bearer form, such as cheques, \npromissory notes, and money order, that are in bearer form or endorsed or made out to a \nfictitious payee, or otherwise that are in such form that title thereto passes upon delivery, \nor incomplete instruments signed but with the payee’s name omitted.  \n15- Beneficial owner : Any natural person who ultimately owns or exercises direct or indirect \ncontrol over a customer or  on whose behal", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-003", "title": "3 ", "content": "3 \n facilitation, or collusion, cover -up or the attempt to commit any of the acts provided for in this \nArticle.  \nArticle 3  \nLegal person  shall be criminally liable for money laundering if any act clarified in Article 2  was \ncommitted in its name or to its  account . Criminal liability of a legal person shall not ex clude \ncriminal liability of the  chairmen,  members of boards of directors, owners, employees, authorized \nrepresentatives, auditors or hired staff , or any other natural person who acted in the legal entity’s \nname or for its credit.  \nArticle 4  \n1- A money laundering offen", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-004", "title": "4 ", "content": "4 \n Article 8 \nFIs and  DNFBPs  shall use appropriate systems to determine whether a customer or beneficial \nowner is or has become assignee with a prominent public function in the Kingdom or a foreign \ncountry; or with a senior  management position in an international organization  and if so, apply \nadditional measures as prescribed by the Implementing Regulation.  \nArticle 9 \n1- Before entering into a cross -border correspondent  relationship,  financial institution s shall apply \nappro priate risk mitigati on measures as prescribed by  the Implementing Regulation,  and shall \nsatisfy themse", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-005", "title": "5 ", "content": "5 \n 3-  In specific cases,  the Public Prosecution may oblige FIs and DNFBPs  to extend the record \nkeeping period for as long as required for the purpose of a criminal investigation or \nprosecution.  \n4- Records shall be sufficient to permit reconstruction of transactions and shall be maintained \nin a manner so that they can be readily ma de available to competent authorities upon \nrequest.   \nArticle 1 3 \nFIs and DNFBPs shall : \n1-  Monitor  and scrutinize transactions , document and data  on an ongoing basis to ensure that \nthey are consistent with the reporting entity’s knowledge of the cu", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-006", "title": "6 ", "content": "6 \n 2- Promptly  and fully respond to requests from the Directorate  for additional information.  \nArticle 16 \n1- FIs, DNFBPs, and NPOs  as well as their Members of Board of Directors,  directors , \nMembers of its exec utive or supervisory management , and employees are prohibited from \ndisclosing to a customer or any other person the fact that a report under this Law or related \ninformation will be, is being or has been submitted to the Directorate , or that a criminal \ninvestigation is being or has been carried out. This shall not preclude disclosures or \ncommunications between directors and", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-007", "title": "7 ", "content": "7 \n disseminate or forward specific information.  \n \nArticle 20 \nEvery person with duties for or within the Directorate  is re quired to keep confidential any \ninformation obtained within the scope of these duties, even after the cessation of those duties.   \nArticle 21 \nInformation disclosed  to the Directorate  may be exchanged  with other  competent authorities . The \nDirectorate  may enter into an agreement or arrangement with competent authorities to facilitate \ncooperation  and information exchange . \nArticle 22 \n1- The Directorate  may, , seek from or share with a foreign counterpart an", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-008", "title": "8 ", "content": "8 \n 5- The Implementing Regulation shall set forth  rules and procedures related to declarations  \nand the powers of General Directorate of Customs to implement the declaration \nobligations .  \n \nVI. Supervision  \nArticle 24 \nSupervisory authorities shall have the following powers and duties to carry out their mandate:  \na. Collect in formation and other data from FIs, DNFBPs, and NPOs as well as  applying  \nappropriate supervisory measures, including on-site inspections and offsite me asures ;  \nb. Compel  FIs, DNFBPs, and NPOs to provide any information that the supervisory authority \nconsid", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-009", "title": "9 ", "content": "9 \n 3. Issue an order to provide regular reports on the measures taken to address the identified \nviolation;  \n4. Impose a m onetary fine of up to 5.000.000 riyals  per violation;  \n5. Ban individuals from employment within the sectors for which the supervisory authority \nhas competences for a period to be determined by the supervisory authority;  \n6. Restrict the powers of directors, boa rd members, executive or supervisory management \nmembers, and controlling owners, including appointing one or more temporary controllers;  \n7. Dismiss or replace the directors, members of the Board of Directo", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-010", "title": "10 ", "content": "10 \n Article 29 \n \nThe court may reduce the sentence of the convicted person  as clarified in Article 30 , if perpetrator  \nreports to authorities  about the money laundering crime , before they have  knowledge, other \nperpetrators to the crime, and if such reporting leads to arresting the perpetrators or seizing crime \ninstrumentalities or proceeds.  \n \nArticle 30 \nPenalties stipulated  in Article 26 may be reduced as per the legal decided circumstances  to be \nimprisonment for a period up to seven  years and  no less than one year, as well as  a fine not \nexceeding three  million riyals or b", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-011", "title": "11 ", "content": "11 \n VIII. Confiscation  \nArticle 33:  \nWithout prejudice to the rights of third parties acting in good faith, i n the event of a conviction \nfor a money laundering or predicate offen ce, the competent Court shall issue an order to \nconfiscate the following:  \na. Laundered funds ; \nb. Proceeds of the crime, including proceeds intermingled with funds  acquired from \nlegitimate source s up to the  value of the intermingled proceeds; and \nc. Instrumentalities.  \n \n2. The competent court shall order the confiscation of funds  regardless of whether the funds are  \nheld or owned by the defendant or ", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-012", "title": "12 ", "content": "12 \n  \nIX. International Cooperation  \nArticle 38 \nCompetent authorities may exchange information  with and make inquiries  or investigation  on \nbehalf of foreign counterparts in countries  that are si gnatories with the Kingdom to valid \nagreements, , or on the basis of reciprocity. This shall be done pursuant to applicable statutory \nprocedures, without prejudice to the  national sovereignty  of the country or  provisions and customs \nrelated to confidentiality of information . \nArticle 39 \nUpon request of a competent court or authority in another country which is a signatory with the \nKing", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-013", "title": "13 ", "content": "13 \n X. Concluding Provisions  \nArticle 43 \n1- the Public Prosecution , either upon its own motion or upon request by the criminal  \ninvestigating officer, may order that any person  or FIs -through the supervisory authority - \nor DNFBPs or NPOs  provide records, document or information . The requested entity shall \nexecute the order as specified in the order , accurately, and  without delay.  The \nImplementing Regulation of this law sh all clarify the relevant mechanisms.   \n2- Whoever  has been served with an order under  Sup (1 ) in this Article shall not disclose to \nany person the existen", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Anti_Money_Launderin-014", "title": "14 ", "content": "14 \n data saved in computers, as specified in the Order, whether for a predicate crime or a ML crime. \nThe order  under this Article should be made ex parte and without prior notice to the party \nconcerned  . \nArticle 47 \nThe competent court shall have jurisdiction to decide on all crimes provided for in this Law.  \n \nArticle 48 \nThe Public Prosecution shall investigate and prosecute crimes stipulated in the Law at the \ncompetent court , and it shall issue s guidelines and instruction to the competent authorities under its \nsupervision by the criminal procedures  law. \nArticle 49 \nCriminal inv", "source": "compliance/Anti-Money Laundering Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-BLK_PDPL_Compliance_-001", "title": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   ", "content": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   \n \n1 NAVIGATING THE LATEST UPDATES IN SAUDI DATA PROTECTION: PDPL \nCOMPLIANCE AND IMPLEMENTATION \n \nI. RECENT UPDATES SINCE AUGUST 2024  \nSince the Personal Data Protection Law ( “PDPL ”) came into effect in September 2023, Saudi Arabia has \nreleased several key updates that significantly impact data protection practices. In August 2024, new \nguidelines and regulations were introduced, setting the stage for compliance in the evolving legal \nlandscape. This article examines these recent updates and provides a comprehensive guide for \nbusinesses to align with the lates", "source": "compliance/BLK_PDPL_Compliance_2024.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-BLK_PDPL_Compliance_-002", "title": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   ", "content": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   \n \n2 Regulation on the Transfer of Personal Data Outside the Kingdom (August 2024)  \nThe Regulation on the Transfer of Personal Data Outside the Kingdom, updated in August 2024, provides \nstricter guidelines for transferring personal data abroad. It outlines that data transfers must meet \nspecific criteria, such as an equivalent level of dat a protection in the receiving country or explicit consent \nfrom the data subject (Regulation on Transfer Outside the Kingdom, Article 3). A key addition is the \nrequirement for a risk assessment before any transfer, ensuring that", "source": "compliance/BLK_PDPL_Compliance_2024.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-BLK_PDPL_Compliance_-003", "title": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   ", "content": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   \n \n3 II. WHAT SHOULD COMPANIES KNOW AND DO?  \nThis article is designed to help companies generally understand the PDPL and the relevant guidelines, \noffering a step -by-step approach to navigate the regulations. Companies must understand the key \ndefinitions and principles in the PDPL which have remained largely unchanged even with the updated \nliterature s urrounding it.  \n \nWhat is Personal Data?  \nUnder the PDPL, personal data refers to any information that can directly or indirectly identify a person, \nincluding names, contact details, national ID numbers, IP add", "source": "compliance/BLK_PDPL_Compliance_2024.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-BLK_PDPL_Compliance_-004", "title": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   ", "content": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   \n \n4 \nDevelop and Maintain a Comprehensive Privacy Policy  \nA compliant privacy policy must cover the following aspects (PDPL Implementing Regulations, Article 7):  \n• Purpose of data collection.  \n• Categories of personal data being processed.  \n• The lawful basis for processing (e.g., consent, contract).  \n• Details of any third -party data sharing.  \n• Rights of the data subject, including access, correction, and deletion rights (PDPL, Article 4).  \n• Information on how to withdraw consent.  \n \nReview and Update Regularly  \nCompanies should regularly review and up", "source": "compliance/BLK_PDPL_Compliance_2024.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-BLK_PDPL_Compliance_-005", "title": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024  ", "content": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024  \n \n5 \nAnswer:  \nYes, ABC Company has breached the PDPL by transferring customer data outside Saudi Arabia without \nadequate safeguards or explicit consent, as required by the law.  \n Step 1: Understanding PDPL Requirements for Cross -Border Data Transfers  \nUnder the PDPL, personal data cannot be transferred outside the Kingdom unless certain conditions are met (PDPL, Article 29). These include:  \n− Explicit consent from the data subject for the transfer.  \n− The receiving country must offer an equivalent level of data protection.  \n− A risk assessment must be conduct", "source": "compliance/BLK_PDPL_Compliance_2024.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-BLK_PDPL_Compliance_-006", "title": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024  ", "content": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024  \n \n6 \nScenario 2: Data Subject Requests for Access and Deletion \nProblem : \nXYZ Insurance, a large insurance company operating in Saudi Arabia, processes the personal data of its \npolicyholders, including sensitive health information. One of XYZ’s customers, Ahmad, requested access to his personal data, including medical records, to review the data that XYZ holds on him. Additionally, Ahmad  has decided to switch insurance providers and has asked XYZ to delete all his personal data, \nciting the fact that he no longer requires the company’s services.  \n XYZ Insurance i", "source": "compliance/BLK_PDPL_Compliance_2024.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-BLK_PDPL_Compliance_-007", "title": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   ", "content": "ARTICLE  • OCTOBER  2024   \n \n7 IV. CONCLUSION  \nNavigating Saudi Arabia’s PDPL and its associated regulations can be complex, but with the right \napproach, and tailored legal advice, companies can ensure compliance while safeguarding personal \ndata. By following the steps outlined in this article —understanding key definitions, securing lawful \nprocessing, protecting data subject rights, and implementi ng strong data security measures —\norganizations can not only avoid penalties but also build trust and demonstrate their commitment to \ndata privacy. As data privacy continues to evolve globall", "source": "compliance/BLK_PDPL_Compliance_2024.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-001", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.\nFulfillment by Amazon \n(FBA) Handbook\nChapter 4\nAmazon pride ourselves on helping Sellers capitalize on global business opportunities to reach out to \nmore quality buyers. With this agenda in mind, Amazon has been continuously innovating and optimizing \nour global logistics system. For example, Amazon Global Logistics (AGL) is aimed to help Sellers ship their \nproducts, whereas Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) is to help Sellers deliver their products into the hands of \ncustomers.\nLet us now learn how to use FBA to ", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-002", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nFulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook4. Using Fulfillment by \nAmazon (FBA) \nHandbook\nHaving studied theearlier contents, doyou have theurge toimmediately start selling products? With\nSeller Central you cancreate and listyour products, send/replenish Inventory toAmazon, track logistics\ninformation, reply tobuyers’ reviews, manage inventory and download analytical data and reports etc.So,\nwhat dothey involve? Letusexamine them oneatatime .\n24.1 Overview of FBA operation\n4.2 Register Amazon Seller account\n4.3 Create p", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-003", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.1Overview of FBA Operation\nAt Seller Central you can complete FBA shipment process using the following steps:\nBefore using Seller Central tocarry out FBA operations you would first\nneed toconfirm thefollowing :\n• Whether theitems shipped tofulfillment centers conform to\nAmazon’s storage policies ;\n• Thequantity ofproducts shipped tofulfillment centers ;\n• The mode ofshipment forfront -leglogistics and logistics\ncarrier ;\n• Whether product preparation isself-handled o", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-004", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.2Register Amazon Seller account\nYou will need to register an Amazon account to be able to use FBA. Click Seller Central to establish an Amazon \naccount (please skip this step if you have already registered).\nYou will probably want to create and list your products as soon as you log onto Seller Central. But before this \nyou will need to check whether Amazon already has the products that you want to sell.\n•If there are such products, you can select direct match by ente", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-005", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.54.3.1 Confirming whether there are existing listings:\nStep 1: Select “Add a Product” in the pull -down menu in “Inventory”;\nStep 2: Search the products that you wish to sell in the search column;\nNote: Searches can be more precise if made using Product name (such as UPC, EAN, ISBN or ASIN)\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-006", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Step 3: Confirm in the search results whether there are products that you intend to sell. \n64.3.2 Add prices for existing products \nAfter you have found the products that you wanted to sell, add their details according to the following steps:\nStep 1: Select your product status;\nSample for reference\nSample for reference\n\nFulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Step 2: Click “Sell this", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-007", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.3.2Create new products\nIfyouhave notsearched theproducts that youintend tosell, youwillneed tocreate anew listing onAmazon .\nBefore creating anew product listing, ensure that youprovide thefollowing information :\n•Product code :The majority ofproducts have anexclusive identification code such asUPC, EAN, JAN\norISBN .Product code allows you tomatch theproduct that you listwith those already listed on\nAmazon, thus avoiding repeated listings aswell asensuring correctnes", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-008", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Step 2: Classify the product. Correct classification of the product will help buyers more easily find your \nproduct; You can select product category using any of the two methods below:\n•Method 1: Select the appropriate product category in “Select category > Browse” option \nframe; Step 1: Click “Inventory > Add a Product > I’m adding a product not sold on Amazon” to initiate creation \nof new listing;\n9\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-009", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.•Method 2: Enter product name in  “Select a product category > Browse” option frame, then \nsingle click “Search icon” button. The system will find matching categories for your selection.\n1.\n2.We hope buyers can shop on Amazon without worries. Therefore we have set restrictions for certain \nspecial products and categories. You will need to add such listings after obtaining “approved \nrequest” on Seller Central. Click here for more details.Note:\n1.Single click the star s", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-010", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.You product listing is ready as soon as you have completed the above information. Your product will \ntypically be listed on Amazon within 15 minutes. Buyers can see your listing price using “browse” and \n“search” functions. When you create a new listing, please use “browse” and “search” functions to check \nwhether you can find the newly -listed product. If you are unable to find it, please refer to Using search \nterms effectively  and How do I find my products ?Step 3:", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-011", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.4 Add Products via Upload\nIn the previous section you have learned about creation of listing by entering such information as product \ncategory, brand ad prices. In this section you will learn how to list products in bulk by using inventory templates \nto list products with different Size and colors etc. Please note that this function applies only to Sellers who have \njoined Professional Selling Plan .\n124.4.1 Creating inventory management template\nStep 1: Click “Inven", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-012", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Step 3: After you have completed Steps 1 and 2, you can generate inventory template. Click “Generate \nTemplate” to download Microsoft Excel form.Step 2: Select the appropriate template. There are three templates to choose from:\n•Simplified version: Includes only relevant fields for the products and essential attributes for \nlisting to be created on Seller Central.\n•Advanced version: Includes all fields (essential, priority and optional attributes) pertaining to \nthe se", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-013", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.4.2 Complete and save inventory template\nFollow the instructions for the inventory template and enter the products’ details. The fields with label in bold \nletters are compulsory. After you have completed the document, save it in Excel format. You have now \ncompleted production of inventory template .\n14\n4.4.3 Upload your inventory file\nAfter you have completed production of inventory template, first check whether it is correctly completed on \nSeller Central, then up", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-014", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.After you have completed uploading you can fill in the inspection report for the inventory file. The \nreport will be sent to your mail box within a few minutes. If you do not wish to enter your mail box, you \ncan click “Check My File”, and check the inspection report on “Add Products via Upload> Monitor \nUploading Status”.Step 2: Click “Choose file” to upload your inventory file in Excel format. \n15\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-015", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Step 5: As in the case with “Check My File”, you will still need to check your file processing report on \n“Monitor Upload Status”. You can check whether there are contents in your inventory file that need to \nrevised;Step 3: You can check whether there are any errors on your file on “Monitor Upload Status”. If there are \nerrors you can correct them and then upload and check the file.\nStep 4: After you have ensured your file is error -free, select the type of file under", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-016", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.5 Convert listings to Fulfilled by Amazon\nAfter your listing is shipped to Amazon, you can convert it into FBA inventory using the following steps, so as to \nallow you to continue carrying out relevant shipment operation on Seller Central.\n17Firstly, click “Manage Inventory” on “Inventory” tab in the menu to access inventory management interface;\nAfter you have accessed inventory management page, look for the products that you wish to convert to FBA \nlisting. You can", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-017", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Pleas remember to select “All” under “Fulfilled By” when carrying out searches.Method 2: You can screen those products that you wish converted to FBA listing through their shipping \nattributes, using “Status” and “ Fulfilled By ” as the main conditions. Of course you can also screen the \nproducts using other conditions (such as beginning and ending dates for search, or prices above or \nbelow certain prices);\n18\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-018", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.After you have found the product using any of the above means, tick“√” in the box to its left. Then click \n“Change to Fulfilled by Amazon” in the “Edit” box on the far right.\nIf you wish to convert all the listings to FBA, select all under “Status” with “√”. Then select “ Change to Fulfilled \nby Amazon ” in “Action on 10 selected” in the pull -down menu;\n19\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-019", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.You will then be redirected to “Add information on dangerous goods”. You will need to confirm in the pop -up \nwindow whether the listings contain batteries or whether they are dangerous items/substances. If you are \nunsure whether your listings are dangerous goods, click here to check and confirm. After you have completed \nthe above click “submit” to return to the earlier page. Select “save and continue”;Once you have clicked “convert to FBA”, you will be directed to t", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-020", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.6Shipment toAmazon/replenishment\nYou can also choose to defer shipment/replenishment. Click “Inventory > Manage Inventory” in the menu and \nselect the editing column to the right of the listing concerned, then click “ Send/Replenishment inventory ”. You \nwill also be redirected to “ Send/replenishment inventory ” page\n21After you have converted your listing to FBA according to the instructions in the previous section, you will be \nredirected to “Send/replenishment in", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-021", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.If you do not wish to have your inventory distributed, you would need to set combined inventory in advance on \nSeller Central. Note that this service is charged on a per -item basis. Please refer to the following for combined \ninventory setting:\nStep 1: Select “Settings > FBA” on Seller Central;Before shipping to Amazon/replenishing your stock, you will need to consider whether to ship the stock to one \nor several fulfillment centers . Please note that if you do not se", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-022", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Clicking “Update” means you have completed combined setting. Next we will proceed to \nshipment/replenishment operation.Step 3: Under “Inventory Placement Option” on Inbound Settings, select “Inventory Placement Service” \nand click “Update”;\n23\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-023", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.6.1 Confirm that the shipment information is correct\nYou need to confirm the shipping address and packaging type are correct in the “Send/replenish inventory” \npage.\n24•The page will automatically set a default shipment address. You need to check and confirm that it is \nthe correct address. You can also click “Ship from another address” to correct your shipment address. \nThe fields containing asterisk (*) are compulsory.\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-024", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.•Asforpackaging type, please select “Individual products” or“Case -packed products” according to\nyour situation .Individual products :There areseveral SKUs inacarton .Case -packed products :There is\nonly oneSKU inacarton .\nAfter youhave confirmed shipping address andpackaging type, click “Continue inventory plan” .\n254.6.2Setting shipment quantity\nEnter thequantity ofitems that youwish inship intheframe, then click “Continue” below it.\nSample for reference\nSample for r", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-025", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.6.4 Labeling\nYou need only to click “Continue” if you wish to select “Amazon” to provide prepping service. If you select \n“seller” you would need to complete the quantity of labels to be printed, as well as the Size of printing paper:4.6.3 Confirming prepping party\nThis page requires you to confirm again the party responsible for product prepping. If you select “seller”, you \nwould have to carry out prepping on your own. If you select “Amazon” we would provide preppi", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-026", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.•By default Amazon will use A4 -size paper for printing. You can also buy adhesive paper in A4 format1\nfor printing. An A4 -size paper typically produces 21~44 labels. You can select the appropriate label \nsize according to the dimensions of packaging. After you have made the selection, click “Print labels \nfor this page”;\n•Below is a screen shot of SKU label. To ensure fuss -free receipt into inventory, you need to fix the \nlabel on the item or packaging. Please remem", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-027", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.•After double -checking, click “Continue” to proceed to the next step.\n284.6.5 Inspection of items\nAfter completing the above we are now ready to check the items. Please check again their shipment address, \npackaging type, the items included in the shipment and item preparation fees etc.:\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-028", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.•On this page you will see “Fulfillment address”. It is the address of FBA warehousing. If you have \npreviously set combined inventory, only the fulfillment address will appear on this page. If you have \nnot set combined inventory Amazon system will automatically distribute the items to several or one \nfulfillment address;\n•Confirm the information and click “Approve & continue”.\n29\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-029", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.6.6 Work on shipment\nAfter you have completed the information as above, you will be directed to the page below. Click “Work on \nShipment” to prepare the items.\n30Step 1: Check the products in the shipment.\nNote: You can check and revise the quantity here, subject to a maximum of 5% of the quantity or 6 \nitems. If you wish to add more products or new products, you must duplicate the shipment or create \nnew shipment. Additional placement fees may be required for any qu", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-030", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Step 3: Package the items. Please select “Everything in one box” or “Several boxes” according to your \nsituation. If you select “ Everything in one box ”,you will need to enter the carton’s Size and weight. See \nCourse: 2.3 Packaging inbound FBA items for more information on packaging requirements. Click here\nto see learning resources guide.Step 2: Select suitable front -leg logistics and fulfillment service;\n•Fulfillment means: The default choice is “small parcel deli", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-031", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Step 4: Print item label. After completing the packaging, use heat -sensitive paper1to print labels and \naffix them on the boxes.If you select “several boxes” you will need to provide correct information on the items in the boxes. You \ncan click any of the means provided on the page to provide information on the items.\n•Use the form on the web page (recommended) and enter details on the boxes.\n•Uploading file: Complete packing list and upload to Seller Central.\n•Skip i", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-032", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.6.7 Tracking items\nYou will be directed to “Track shipment” page after you have completed the above steps. Here you should enter \nthe waybill number for the items provided by the carrier. Click “Save All” to track the items’ logistics \ninformation.\n33You can also elect to enter the waybill number later. Click “Inventory > Management FBA inventory > Shipping \nQueue” on the menu. On the edit column to the right of the item concerned, click “Track shipment” to enter the", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-033", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.7FBA Seller Operation, Management and Analysis\n4.7.1Management ofInventory Health\nByusing thetools inyour Seller account, you canmanage excess inventory easily and letproducts with less\nattention obtain greater opportunity forexposure .You can also reduce storage fees and improve storage\nefficiency byusing inventory removal .\n•Manage excess inventory\nAmazon helps you toeasily confirm excess inventory that may exist, and provides you with suggested\nmeasures forbetter ", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-034", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.•Create removal order\nYou can create removal orders in two ways:\nMethod 1: Click “Inventory > Management inventory > Create removal order” and select the \ninventory to be removed. If you wish for the products to be returned to you, please provide the \nreceiving address and complete the following information; if you wish to discard them, please \nselect “Disposal”;\n35Method 2: Click “Data report > Inventory and sales report > Recommend removals”. Under \nrecommended remov", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-035", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.7.2Replying andprocessing buyer reviews\nFBA provides 7x24hour customer service .You canalso reply tobuyers’ questions onspecific product through\n“buyer -seller messages ”,thereby giving buyers better shopping experience .\n36•First ofall,click “Setting >FBA” onSeller Central ;\n•Then click “Product Support >Edit” Respectively ;\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-036", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.•Select “Enable”, then click “Update”;\n37If after following the above instructions you still have not found messages that you wish to reply, click “Buyer \nMessages” to enter message -reply page. Select “Response Needed” for the pull -down list, then click the \nrelevant message for your reply.\nSample for reference\nSample for reference", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-037", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.•Downloading business report\nSelect the checkbox on the left and click “Download” to download data report in CSV format.•Check your business report (based on data, ASIN and others)\nClick “Report > Business report s” on the menu to view your business report. In the navigation column on \nthe left of the page there is a series of reports organized according to [date], [ASIN] and [others]. You \ncan click on the option to view the data.4.7.3 Checking and downloading FBA dat", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-038", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.4.8Terminologies and Definitions\n39Terminology Definition\nAdhesive paperItismainly made using white -board paper with high tensile strength coated\nwith adhesive .Itismostly used toprint labels .\nHeat -sensitive printing \npaperItisatype ofprocessed paper .Alayer of“heat -sensitive” adhesive (heat -\nsensitive color -changing layer) iscoated onquality raw paper .Itistypically\nused toproduce labels .", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-039", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Related Topics", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook –Related Topics\nCopyright ©  2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.Fulfillment by Amazon ( FBA) services\n•What is FBA?\n•Preparation before FBA storage\n•Amazon FBA inventory management\n•Using Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook\n•Flexible and diverse FBA solutions\n40Related Topics\nLearn more about related courses:\n•Search “cross -border logistics” for essential knowledge for new Sellers\n•How to carry out first -leg transportation for FBA inbound cross -border logistics? Search “ AGL”\n•You can also carry out self -fulfillment of your products, search “ FBM/MFN ”\nP", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-CN_GS_FBA_1_4_Guidan-040", "title": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook -Learning Feedback", "content": "Fulfillment by Amazon (FBA) Handbook -Learning Feedback\nCopyright © 2021 Amazon. All rights reserved.\nLearning Feedback\n41If you found the content covered to be helpful for you, please light up the “       ” in the bottom right corner \nof the page to give your feedback, thank you!", "source": "compliance/CN_GS_FBA_1.4_Guidance_EN.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Cloud_Computing_Ecom-001", "title": "© 202 3 Ibrahim Alfadli . This open -access article is distributed under a Creat", "content": "© 202 3 Ibrahim Alfadli . This open -access article is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution (CC -BY) 4.0 \nlicense.  Journal of Computer Science  \n \n \n \nOriginal Research Paper  \nCloud Computing Model for E-Commerce in Saudi Arabia  \n \nIbrahim Alfadli  \n \nDepartment of Information Systems, College of Computer Science and Engineering, Taibah University, Medina, Saudi Arabia  \n \nArticle history  \nReceived: 20-12-2022  \nRevised: 15-01-2023  \nAccepted:  19-01-2023  \n \nEmail:  ialfadli@taibahu.edu.sa  Abstract:  The impact of cloud computing can be seen in several industries, \nincluding ", "source": "compliance/Cloud_Computing_Ecommerce_Saudi.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Cloud_Computing_Ecom-002", "title": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 ", "content": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 \nDOI: 10.3844/jcssp.2023 .446.453 \n \n447  \n \nFig. 1:  Types of E-commerce  \n \nTherefore, it is crucial to comprehend why some \nbusinesses are better equipped than others to make the \nswitch to the cloud to promote the adoption of cloud \ntechnology. There are numerous E-commerce  models \nthat offer a range of services, in Fig . 1. These models \ninclude business to customer, customer to customer, \ncustomer to business  and government to customer.  \nThus, this  study proposed a cloud computing model for \nE-commerce  in SA utili", "source": "compliance/Cloud_Computing_Ecommerce_Saudi.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Cloud_Computing_Ecom-003", "title": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 ", "content": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 \nDOI: 10.3844/jcssp.2023 .446.453 \n \n448 The articles selected were filtered based on research \nobjectives outlined. First screening aimed at the titles and \nabstracts of the articles, then the conclusions  and eventually \nthe entire article content. For this purpose, a selection of \ninclusion/exclusion criteria were adhered to as follows:  \n \na) Inclusion criteria  include articles o n cloud computing \nadoption for E-commerce  in developing countries \nsuch as the proposed architecture framework, its \nadoption level, benefit", "source": "compliance/Cloud_Computing_Ecommerce_Saudi.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Cloud_Computing_Ecom-004", "title": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 ", "content": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 \nDOI: 10.3844/jcssp.2023 .446.453 \n \n449 Table 3: The extracted common terminologies  \nRefs Extracted terminologies (common lay ers, processes, concepts, tasks)  \nCai et al. (2009); Sim (2009); Neumann et al. (2010); Yang et al. Reduce equipment needs, maintainability, connectivity, lack of infrastructure  \n(2010); Cai et al. (2011); Al -Jaberi et al. (2015); (Busalim and  in the rural area, limited internet access, sustainability, cost reduction, cost of  \nHussin (2015); Budiono et al. (2018) ; Shaikh and Patil (2014);  imp", "source": "compliance/Cloud_Computing_Ecommerce_Saudi.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Cloud_Computing_Ecom-005", "title": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 ", "content": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 \nDOI: 10.3844/jcssp.2023 .446.453 \n \n450 Islamic area with the biggest budget ($168 billion) \namongst the Arab territories with nearly no immediate \ncharges. W ith a high revenue ranked at 9000 buying \npower equivalen ce (associated to the local and \ninternational means of 6167 and 7416) and a great yearly \npeople increase rate of 3.4%, Saudi culture offers a possibly \nwide customer base for business and knowledge dri ven \nenterprises. More, the major youth people and the existence \nof huge IT businesses in the Sa provide E-", "source": "compliance/Cloud_Computing_Ecommerce_Saudi.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Cloud_Computing_Ecom-006", "title": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 ", "content": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 \nDOI: 10.3844/jcssp.2023 .446.453 \n \n451 Albugmi, A. N. (2018).  A model for cloud computing \nadoption by Saudi government overseas agencies  \n(Doctoral dissertation, University of Southampton).  \nhttp://eprints.soton.ac.uk/id/eprint/424430  \nAl-Dhaqm, A. M. R., Othman, S. H., Abd Razak, S., & \nNgadi, A. (2014, August). Towards adapting \nmetamodelling technique for database forensics \ninvestigation domain. In  2014 International \nSymposium on Biometrics a nd Security Technologies \n(ISBAST)  (pp: 322-327). IEEE.   \n https://d", "source": "compliance/Cloud_Computing_Ecommerce_Saudi.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Cloud_Computing_Ecom-007", "title": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 ", "content": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 \nDOI: 10.3844/jcssp.2023 .446.453 \n \n452 Alyoubi, B. A. (2021). Clustering Analysis of Logistics \nPerformance in Saudi Arabia: A Roadmap to Cloud \nComputing and IoT & Blockchain Solutions.  \nInternational Transaction Journal of Engineering, \nManagement, & Applied Sciences & Technologies , \n12(7), 1 -14.  \n https://tuengr.com/V12/12A7G.pdf  \nAulkemeier, F., Paramartha, M. A., Iacob, M. E., & van \nHillegersberg, J. (2016). A pluggable service platform \narchitecture for E-commerce . Information Systems \nand e-Business Managemen", "source": "compliance/Cloud_Computing_Ecommerce_Saudi.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Cloud_Computing_Ecom-008", "title": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 ", "content": "Ibrahim Alfadli  / Journal of Computer Science 2023 , 19 (4): 446.453 \nDOI: 10.3844/jcssp.2023 .446.453 \n \n453 Qasem, Y. A., Abdullah, R., Yah, Y.,  Atan, R., Al -Sharafi, \nM. A., & Al -Emran, M. (2021). Towards the \ndevelopment of a comprehensive theoretical model \nfor examining the cloud computing adoption at the \norganizational level.  Recent Advances in Intelligent \nSystems and Smart Applications , 63-74. \nhttps://doi.org/ 10.1007/978 -3-030-47411 -9_4 \nQwaider, W. Q. (2020). A Conceptual Model for E-\ncommerce  Applications based a Cloud \nComputing.  International Journal of Computer \nAppl", "source": "compliance/Cloud_Computing_Ecommerce_Saudi.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-001", "title": "Law  on Combating the Financing  of Terrorism  ", "content": "Law  on Combating the Financing  of Terrorism  \n \nChapter  One \n \nDefinitions  \n \n \n \nArticle  1 \n \nThe following  terms  and phrases  - wherever  mentioned  in this Law - shall  have the \nmeanings  assigned  thereto  unless  the context  requires  otherwise:  \n \n1. Law: Combating Terrorism  Crimes and their Financing  Law \n \n2. Regulation : Implementing Regulation  of the Law  \n \n3. Terrorist Crime : Any act committed,  individually  or collectively,  directly  or indirectly,  by a \nperpetrator,  with the intention  to disturb  public  order,  destabilize  national  security  or state stabili", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-002", "title": "commercial papers , securities,  or  any interest, p rofit or other income gener", "content": "commercial papers , securities,  or  any interest, p rofit or other income generated from such funds . \n \n9. Proceeds of Crime: the funds directly or indirectly obtained or acquired from or through the \ncommission of a predicate offense, whether inside the Kingdom or outside, including any funds \ntransferred or c onverted wholly or partially into other type of funds  \n \n10. Instrumentalities:  Anyt hing prepared,  used, or intended to  use, in any form to commit a crime of \ncrimes stipulated in the law .  \n \n11. Provisional  Seizure : Temporary ban on the transport, transfer, e xchange, conver", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-003", "title": "a customer or on whose behalf a transaction is being conducted, including on the", "content": "a customer or on whose behalf a transaction is being conducted, including on the FIs or DNFBPs or NPOs \nor any  other legal person . \n \n21. Competent  Authority: Any administrative authority, law enforcement authority,  control authority  or \nsupervisory authority with competences in the area s of combating terrorism and its financing , induction, \nsearch, attachment, freezing., arrest, investigation, public prose cution, trial, as per the context, in \naccordance with this Law.   \n \n22. Supervisory Authority : The authority with responsibility to verify  compliance by FIs, DNFBPs, and \nNPOs  w", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-004", "title": "Article  5  ", "content": "Article  5  \nThe Public Prosecution  may issue  a warrant  of arrest  or summon any person  suspected of  committing  a crime  \nprovided for  in this Law.  \nIn all cases, the arrested person may not be held for more than (7) days except by written order , as per the \nprocedures and requirement specified by the implementing  regulation . \n \nArticle  6  \n1. The Public Prosecution  may at its own motion  or upon the request of the criminal investigating \nofficer , order that  any person,  a financial institution , designated none -financial businesses or non -\nprofit organiz ation  provide record", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-005", "title": "suspected to be connected to any  of the crimes set forth in this Law,  and will", "content": "suspected to be connected to any  of the crimes set forth in this Law,  and will be used therein and \nwhich will be subject to confiscation.  \n \n2- The President of the State Security may,  at the stage of deduction, order the competent authority \nto urgently effect a provisional seizure , with prior notice to  the party concerned, on funds, \ninstrumentalities or proceeds suspected of being connected with any of the crimes stated in this \nLaw, or will be used therein and shall be subject to confiscation . Notification to the Public \nProsecution  shall be made withi n a limited time not exceedi", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-006", "title": "Article  16: ", "content": "Article  16: \nA person accused or convicted of one of the crimes provided for in this Law, who sustains damage due to \nprolonged detention or imprisonment, may petition the President of the State Security seeking \nindemnifica tion prior to appearing  before the specialized criminal court. The petition shall be reviewed \nby a settlement committee formed for such purpose, pursuant to a decision by the president . Said \ncommittee shall comprise at least three members, including a Shar ia counselor and a legal counselor. \nCommittee decisions shall be taken by majority within a period not exceeding", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-007", "title": "The civil claimant  may file his case before  the specialized  criminal court  a", "content": "The civil claimant  may file his case before  the specialized  criminal court  after the completion  of the \ninvestigation  relating  to public right.  \n \nArticle 24 \nThe specialized  criminal  court  shall  decide  on the following : \n1-  Crimes  set forth  in this Law. \n2-  Claims  for annulment  of decisions  and claims  for compensation  in connection  with the application  \nof this Law.  \n3- Requests for the execution of final foreign judgments relating to any terrorist offense or terrorist \nfinancing offense, including those relating to the confiscation of funds, proceeds or instruments ", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-008", "title": "- in any of the crimes provided for in this Law, he shall be placed in a special", "content": "- in any of the crimes provided for in this Law, he shall be placed in a specialized treatment center, provided \nthat wh en the absence of symptoms of incapacity is presented to the Public Prosecution to consider the \nresumption of the prosecution . \nChapter 4  \n \nSanctions  \n \n \nArticle 30 \nWhoever describes, explicitly or implicitly, the King or the Crown Prince as infidel, or challe nges him in \nhis religion or justice shall be sentenced to no more than a ten -year prison term of no less than a five -year \nterm.  \nArticle 31 \nWhoever carries a weapon or explosives for executing a terrorist ", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-009", "title": "this purpose his competence , power or responsibility  over him or any other  ed", "content": "this purpose his competence , power or responsibility  over him or any other  educational, training, guidance, \nsocial, instructive or information capacity the punishment shall not be less than fifteen years.  \nArticle 36 \nWhoever performs any of the following acts – for committing any of the crimes se t forth under this Law - \nshall be sentenced to no more than a twenty -year prison term or no less than ten years:  \n1. Dedicates , prepares or manages a place for training.  \n2. Trains , receives trains or finance training in any of the following:  \na) Use of  any weapons, explosives, nuclear, ", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-010", "title": "using  of weapons or explosives for threatening.  ", "content": "using  of weapons or explosives for threatening.  \nArticle 41 \nWhoever highjacks any means of air, sea or land transport or threatens to carry out any of such acts for \ncommitting a terrorist crime or terrorism financing offence shall be sentenced to no more than  a thirty -year \nprison term or no less than a ten -year term. The court may sentenced to death if any of such deeds was \naccompanied with the use of or threatening with weapons or explosives.  \nArticle 42 \nWhoever destroys any of the public or private utilities or any means of transport, or any air, sea or land \ncompanies or platform", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-011", "title": "funds have not been used, shall  be sentenced to no more than fifteen -year pris", "content": "funds have not been used, shall  be sentenced to no more than fifteen -year prison term and no less than a \nfive-year term. If the doer has exploited, for this purpose, the facilities empowered to him by his job capacity \nor his professional activity or social standing, the punishment shall  not be less a ten -year prison term.  \nArticle 48 \nWhoever has traveled to another State for the purpose of committing any of the offenses set out in this Law \nshall be punished by imprisonment for a term not exceeding fifteen years and not less than five year s. \nArticle 49 \nAny legal person whose any of ", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-012", "title": "Article 52 ", "content": "Article 52 \nNone of the punishments prescribed under this Law shall prejudice a more severe punishment based on the \nprovisions of Islamic Sharia Law or other laws.  \nArticle 53 \n1. A Saudi citizen, convicted for imprisonment in cases related to the crimes stated in this Law, shall \nbe banned from travel outside the Kingdom - on completion of his prison term - for a period similar \nto the punishment term of which he had been convicted.  \n2. A no n-Saudi person, convicted of a prison term in cases related to the crime s stated in this Law, \nshall be repatriated from the Kingdom upon the complet", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-013", "title": "If the convict returns to commit any of the offenses set out in the provisions o", "content": "If the convict returns to commit any of the offenses set out in the provisions of the Law, the  suspension of \nexecution shall be suspended without prejudice to the penalty prescribed for the new offense.  \n \nChapter 5  \nConfiscation  \n \nArticle 58  \nWithout prejudice  to the rights  of third  persons  in good faith, the Court shall in the event of a conviction ; \nissue an order to confiscat e the following : \n \na. Proceeds of the crime, including proceeds intermingled with funds acquired from legitimate \nsource s up to the value of the intermingled proceeds;  \nb. Instrumentalities; and  \nc. F", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-014", "title": "Chapter Six ", "content": "Chapter Six \nPreventive Measures  \nArticle 63 \nFIs, DNFBPs, and NPOs  shall identify, assess,  understand and  document its financing of terrorism r isks, \ntaking into account a wide range of risk factors, including those relating to its customers, countries or \ngeographic areas, products, services, transactions and delivery cha nnels , and provide its risk assessment  \nreport to the supervisory authorities upon request. The risk assessment under this Article shall include an \nassessment, prior to their use, of the risks associated with new products, business practices and technologie s.   \n \n", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-015", "title": "Article 69  ", "content": "Article 69  \nFIs and DNFBPs must m onitor and scruti nize transactions , documents , and data  on an ongoing basis  as \nprescribed by  the relevant provisions stipulated in the  Anti-Money Laundering Law , and e xamine any \ncomplex and unusual large transaction, and any unusual pattern of transactions that has no clear economic \nor legal o bjective.  \nArticle 70 \nFIs, DNFBPs, and NPOs  including the providers of legal and account services  that suspect or has reasonable \ngrounds to susp ect that funds or parts thereof  are related or linked to or be used for financing o f terrorism,  \nincludin", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-016", "title": "Article 73 ", "content": "Article 73 \nAn accu sed person  or convicted of a crime set out in the law may be extradited to  and from  another state \nprovided  that such extradition is pursuant to a valid agreement between the Kingdom and the requesting \nstate, or on the basis of reciprocity. If the extr adition request is denied, he shall be tried before the competent \ncourts in the Kingdom, using investigations provided by the state requesting extradition.  \n \nArticle 74: \nThe Committee for Mutual Legal Assistance shall receive and process requests for mutual legal assistance \nconcerning crimes set out  in this law . \n", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-017", "title": "Article 79 ", "content": "Article 79 \nThe Directorate  may, on its ow n motion  or by request , disseminate information and the results of its analysis \nto relevant competent authorities when there are grounds to suspect t hat a transaction is related to  financing \nof terrorism . The Directorate shall have the authority to carry out its function freely, including the \nautonomous decision to conduct analysis , request, disseminate or forward specific information.  \n \nArticle 80 \nInformation disclosed to The Directorate may be exchanged  with competent authorities .  \nArticle 81 \n1- The Directorate  may seek from or sha", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-018", "title": "7. Establish and apply effective fit and proper screening procedures for any per", "content": "7. Establish and apply effective fit and proper screening procedures for any person aiming to participate \nin the management or supervision of the FIs, DNFBPs, and NPOs  or for any person aiming to own or \ncontrol, directly or indirectly, or becoming a beneficial owner of significant shares of a reporting entity; \nand \n8. Maintain statistics concerning any measure adopted and sanction imposed.  \n \nArticle 83 \n \nWithout prejudice to any stricter sanctions and subject to the procedures provided for in other laws, if  the \nsupervisory authority  find that FIs, DNFBPs, and NPOs  or any of their di", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-019", "title": "2- Provide the necessary protection to witnesses, sources, judges, prosecutors, ", "content": "2- Provide the necessary protection to witnesses, sources, judges, prosecutors, investigators, \ndefense counsel and others in the event of serious reasons that endanger their lives, safety, \nbasic interests or family members  to damage or danger.  \nThe Implementing Regulation shall set th e proper mechanism therein.  \n \nArticle 86 \nCompetent authorities  in the kingdom  may exchange information with each other that are released by FIs, \nDNFBPs, and NPOs . This shall be done pursuant to applicable statutory procedures, without prejudice to \nthe provi sions and customs related to confidentiality", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
    {"id": "COMPLIANCE-Combating_Terrorism_-020", "title": "Article 93 ", "content": "Article 93 \nThe provisions of the Criminal Procedures Law shall apply in cases not provided for in this Law.  \n \nArticle 94  \nThis law shall replace the law of terrorist crimes and its financing, issued by Roy al Decree No. (M / 16) \ndated 24/2/1435 AH, and repeal any contrary provisions.  \n \nArticle 95 \nThe Council of ministers  shall issue Implementing Regulations after the preparation of state security \npresidency, public prosecution, ministry of justice, and Minist ry of Finance  within no more than (180) days . \n \nArticle 96  \nThis Law shall enter into force on the day following the date ", "source": "compliance/Combating Terrorism and Financing of Terrorism Law.pdf", "dimension": "compliance", "tags": []},
